    <acqinfo><p>Donated by Peiter G. Buning (donor no. <num type="donor" encodinganalog="541$e">12216</num>) in July 2021 and November 2022.</p></acqinfo>
    <bioghist><p> Harm Buning (1922-2006) was a Professor of Aerospace Engineering in the University of Michigan School of Engineering. Buning was born in The Hague, Netherlands, and grew up in Holland before immigrating to the United State following World War II. He earned bachelor's and master's degrees in aerospace engineering from the University of Michigan in1949 and 1951, respectively. He began his teaching career at Oregon State College in 1954, and joined the faculty at the University of Michigan in 1956, achieving the rank of professor in 1963. In the Department of Aerospace Engineering, Buning was the undergraduate program advisor and served as the department's associate chair from 1966 until his retirement in 1992.</p><p> Buning's primary interest was manned space flight, including mission analysis, aircraft performance, and space systems design. At U-M his courses on spacecraft design were supported by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) through the Universities Space Research Association and gave many students hands-on opportunities to contribute to major spacecraft design efforts. The University recognized his efforts as an educator with a Thurnau Professorship in 1988. Outside U-M, Buning gave courses on orbital and flight mechanics to the early astronauts of the 1960s at NASA's Manned Spacecraft Center. He also served visiting professorships at the University of Arizona in 1962 and at the Technical University Delft in the Netherlands in 1988.</p><p> Buning spent the years following his retirement at his private island in Canada and reconnecting with family in Holland and Norway.</p></bioghist>
    <relatedmaterial><p>The Bentley Historical Library houses the following related collections: <title xlink:href="https://findingaids.lib.umich.edu/catalog/umich-bhl-2011113" xlink:show="new" xlink:actuate="onRequest">College of Engineering (University of Michigan) Oral History Interviews</title>, which includes an interview with Harm Buning.</p></relatedmaterial>
    <scopecontent><p>This collection consists of the professional records of Harm Buning, primarily documenting his activities at the University of Michigan and at NASA. Materials include correspondence, course materials, photographs and prints, realia, and reel-to-reel audiotapes, clippings, and more. Much of the NASA material pertains to Buning's teaching of astronauts, as well as the Gemini and Apollo missions more generally.</p></scopecontent>
